# Why does the second batch of pancakes brown much faster?

My wife and I have been making pancakes together for a while now. We make a large batch of pancakes and cook them one after the other. I noticed that the first batch of pancakes take much longer to brown, and the second batch takes much less time to brown.

I thought that this might be due to the fact that the pan is hotter after the first batch, but the second batch still takes much less time to cook.

What is the cause of this phenomenon?

-
How thick are the pancakes? – J. M. Oct 14 '11 at 21:50
@J.M. The pancakes are about 3mm thick – user7733 Oct 14 '11 at 21:54
I'd guess that the temperature of the pan is a factor. – J. M. Oct 14 '11 at 21:55
@J.M. The pan is hotter after the first batch. – user7733 Oct 14 '11 at 21:56
But not by that much. – J. M. Oct 14 '11 at 21:57

## 1 Answer

I'm not sure how much this helps, but I've noticed this phenomenon when cooking eggs. I think it's because the egg proteins in the first batch have a chance to coagulate, thus leaving more room for the next batch. I suspect a similar thing is happening to the pancakes.

-
That makes sense. I'm not sure how the proteins in the first batch of pancakes have a chance to coagulate before the next batch is put in, though. – user7733 Oct 14 '11 at 21:59
@user7733: They don't. The coagulation occurs when the second batch is in the pan. – J. M. Oct 14 '11 at 22:00
@J.M. That's what I thought. – user7733 Oct 14 '11 at 22:01
@user7733: I think the first batch gives the pan a head start in heating up. – J. M. Oct 14 '11 at 22:03
@J.M. I'm not sure about that. I think that the first batch is cooked at the same temperature as the second batch. – user7733 Oct 14 '11 at 22:05

