class Foo {
  bar: string | number;
  constructor(bar: string | number) {
    this.bar = bar;
  }
  printBar() {
    console.log(this.bar); // This doesn't work
  }
  printBarAsNumber() {
    console.log(this.bar as number); // This works
  }
}

I would like to have printBar() work only when bar is a string, but I would like to use the property as a number in printBarAsNumber(). Is there any way to do this?

A solution with generics might look like this, but it doesn't work because I can't restrict the type of the constructor argument to a single type:

class Foo<T extends string | number> {
  bar: T;
  constructor(bar: T) {
    this.bar = bar;
  }
  printBar() {
    console.log(this.bar);
  }
  printBarAsNumber() {
    console.log(this.bar as number); // This doesn't work
  }
}

new Foo(5).printBarAsNumber(); // error

You can use an overload:

class Foo {
  bar: string | number;
  constructor(bar: string | number) {
    this.bar = bar;
  }
  printBar(): void;
  printBar(): void {
    console.log(this.bar); // This doesn't work
  }
  printBarAsNumber(): void;
  printBarAsNumber() {
    console.log(this.bar as number); // This works
  }
}

This is a workaround but it's not ideal because the compiler doesn't know that printBarAsNumber will only be called when bar is a number. If I change bar to be a number and call printBarAsNumber() I get a runtime error.

