What are the main reasons why most physicists do not believe in the many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics? I know that it is a valid interpretation but I don't know why most people don't like it. Does it have something to do with the fact that it is not testable?

-
Related: physics.stackexchange.com/q/2205/2451 – Qmechanic Feb 12 '13 at 14:55

The main reason is that the many-worlds interpretation is not an interpretation. It's a picture of what the mathematics means, and as such it is untestable. But the mathematics of quantum mechanics is very precisely testable. It is not just a matter of personal preference which picture you use. Many of the other interpretations are better pictures, because they are consistent with the mathematics, and they also give some insight into what the mathematics means.

-
So you are saying that it is just a picture of what the mathematics means? But what is the difference between the Copenhagen interpretation and many-worlds? – user13107 Feb 12 '13 at 15:43
@user13107: The Copenhagen interpretation is a picture that is consistent with the mathematics. The many-worlds interpretation is not a consistent picture. – Peter Shor Feb 12 '13 at 15:44
@user13107: The difference is that the Copenhagen interpretation is a picture that is consistent with the mathematics, while many-worlds is not. – Peter Shor Feb 12 '13 at 15:44
@user13107: It's not a matter of "opinion". The Copenhagen interpretation is a consistent picture, the many-worlds interpretation is not. The Copenhagen interpretation is the one that has been used to make predictions that have been tested, and has been confirmed by experiment. – Peter Shor Feb 12 '13 at 15:49
@user13107: The many-worlds interpretation is not a picture of the mathematics. It's a picture of the mathematics. The Copenhagen interpretation is a picture of the mathematics. – Peter Shor Feb 12 '13 at 15:51

The many-worlds interpretation is not a scientific theory. It is a philosophical position about the meaning of the mathematical formalism of quantum mechanics. As such, it is not testable. It does not make any predictions that can be tested against experiment.

