Develop a C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file.

The program must:

• Read the file line by line
• Ignore lines that start with a hash symbol (#)
• Count the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file
• Display the number of occurrences of the word on the screen

The program must use a loop to read the file. The user must enter the word to search for, and the program must display the number of occurrences of that word on the screen.

The program must use the following functions:

• getline() to read each line of the file
• string::find() to check if a string exists in a line
• string::substr() to extract a substring from a line
• string::length() to determine the length of a line

Here is an example of how the program should work:

Enter the name of the file to search: test.txt
Enter the word to search for: hello
The word "hello" occurs 3 times in the file.

Solution: Here is a possible implementation of this program in C++. Note that this is just one way to solve this problem, there may be other approaches as well.

    #include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Declare variables
    string filename, word;
    int count = 0;

    // Prompt user to enter filename and word to search for
    cout << "Enter the name of the file to search: ";
    getline(cin, filename);
    cout << "Enter the word to search for: ";
    getline(cin, word);

    // Open the file for reading
    ifstream infile(filename);
    if (!infile) {
        cerr << "Error opening file" << endl;
        return -1;
    }

    // Read each line of the file and check if it contains the word
    string line;
    while (getline(infile, line)) {
        size_t pos = line.find(word);
        while (pos!= string::npos) {
            count++;
            pos = line.find(word, pos + 1);
        }
    }

    // Close the file
    infile.close();

    // Display the result
    cout << "The word \"" << word << "\" occurs " << count << " times in the file." << endl;

    return 0;
}

